      "The Pico de Orizaba, or Citlaltépetl, rises to more than 5,600 m, the highest mountain in Mexico and the third highest in North America, after Denali and Mount Logan. It is a dormant volcano, its glaciers shrinking fast, and it is climbed by mountaineers from around the world. It last erupted in the mid-nineteenth century.",
      "The Palacio de Hierro, the Iron Palace, on Orizaba's main square, is a building of iron and steel prefabricated in Belgium and shipped to Mexico in pieces in the 1890s. It is often attributed to the workshop of Gustave Eiffel, the designer of the Eiffel Tower, and it now houses museums, including one devoted to beer.",
      "On the night of 13 to 14 June 1862, a few weeks after the Mexican victory at Puebla, French troops climbed the Cerro del Borrego, the hill overlooking Orizaba, and surprised a Mexican force camped on its summit, routing it. The battle helped the French hold Orizaba as their base until they returned to take Puebla the following year.",
      "Orizaba's rainy climate comes from its position on the slopes facing the Gulf of Mexico. Moist air from the sea rises up the mountains, cooling and condensing into clouds and rain, which fall on the valley for much of the year, while the high plateau beyond the mountains, in the rain shadow of the Pico de Orizaba, is dry and cold.",
